<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tübingen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tübingen is a university town on the River <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. Its university, founded in 1477, and the Protestant seminary known as the Stift trained generations of Württemberg clergy and officials, including Hegel, Hölderlin and Schelling. In the nineteenth century the Tübingen school of theology under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-christian-baur">Ferdinand Christian Baur</a> shaped modern biblical criticism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tubingen.jpg" alt="Tübingen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dktue · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altstadt-tuebingen-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tübingen is a traditional university city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, 30 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. It grew around the base of the fortress Schloss Hohentübingen and of the St. George's Collegiate Church, on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> and Ammer rivers. The Neckar flows east through the city, just south of the medieval old town. To the north lies the Schönbuch, a densely wooded nature park, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Alb</a> rises to the south-east. The Schlossberg and the Österberg stand in the city centre, and large parts of the city are hilly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was probably first settled in the 12th millennium BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> left traces in 85 CE, when they built a limes frontier wall at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> in confrontation with the Alamanni. Records of the castle of Hohentübingen go back to 1078, when Henry IV, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, besieged it. In the middle of the 12th century the Counts of Zollern were raised to Imperial Counts Palatine, with their seat at Tübingen. By 1231 it was a civitas, which meant the Crown recognised its civil liberties, a market and a court system.</p><p>In the later 13th century the town gained Augustinian and Franciscan monasteries and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> school, today's Uhland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a>. In 1342 the town and castle passed to the Counts of Württemberg. The clock tower of the Collegiate Church was begun in 1411. The Jewish community, centred on the Judengasse below the Rathaus, was expelled in 1477, the year the university was founded.</p><p>The university, Eberhard Karl University, is one of the oldest north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. In the 19th century it was associated with the German-patriotic student Burschenschaften, whose large fraternity houses remain a feature of the town. It was associated with the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">National Socialism</a> in the years between the world wars. Among those linked with the town are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a>, the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a>, and Wilhelm Schickard, professor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and astronomy and inventor of a mechanical calculator. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel">Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel</a> and Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling studied there, and Friedrich Hölderlin studied, lived and died in the town, as did the theologian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-christian-baur">Ferdinand Christian Baur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tübingen survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> with its historic fabric almost wholly intact, and its old town, with crooked cobblestone lanes, half-timbered houses and the Rathaus on the central market, is one of the few Altstädte in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> that is completely intact. Students make up almost a third of its 90,000 residents. After the 1960s the city expanded on the high ground to the north, and later into the Loretto and French Quarter on land that French military bases had left. It is the capital of a district and of an administrative region, and the university is its largest employer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tübingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Württemberg became one of the leading constitutional states of southern Germany, with an efficient, university-trained civil service drawn largely from <b>Tübingen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Hugo, a younger son of the Palatine Count of <b>Tübingen</b>, received lands in Rhaetia around 1200 and named himself after Montfort castle in Vorarlberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-montfort">County of Montfort</a></li><li><blockquote>A collegiate foundation existed at Marchtal from the 8th century, and in 1171 Count Palatine Hugh of <b>Tübingen</b> refounded it for Premonstratensian canons.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-marchtal">Imperial Abbey of Marchtal</a></li></ul></section>
